# Python best practices

## Style & clarity (PEP 8 spirit)

- Readable names; one concept per function; keep functions short
- Prefer explicit over clever; avoid deeply nested blocks
- Match existing project style (formatter/linter if present)

## Typing

- Annotate public functions and Pydantic/FastAPI boundaries
- Prefer `list[str]`, `dict[str, Any]`, `X | None` (3.10+) over legacy `typing` aliases when consistent with the file
- Do not add noisy types that fight the type checker without benefit

## Errors & control flow

- Raise specific exceptions with clear English messages
- Avoid bare `except:`; catch the narrowest practical type
- Do not swallow errors silently; log or re-raise with context
- Fail fast on invalid configuration at startup

## Data & APIs

- Validate at the edge (Pydantic / schemas); keep core logic pure when possible
- Immutability by default for shared config; no hidden global mutable state
- Use context managers for resources (files, clients)

## Dependencies & modules

- Standard library first; add third-party deps only when needed
- Clear package layout; avoid circular imports
- No star imports (`from x import *`) in application code

## Testing & change hygiene

- Behavior changes need tests (see `testing-*.mdc`)
- Do not commit `.env`, secrets, or large generated artifacts
- Prefer small, reviewable diffs

## FastAPI specifics in this repo

- Routers thin; services own LLM I/O
- Document endpoints for OpenAPI (`openapi-docs.mdc`)
- Docstrings Sphinx-style (`python-docstrings.mdc`)
